# PART 2 — BUILD AN AI NOTES GENERATION ENGINE

Create a dedicated notes-generation engine that turns a student's topic, chapter, uploaded material, or selected syllabus section into comprehensive handwritten study notes.

The notes must not be short generic summaries. They must explain the subject thoroughly, progressively, and accurately.

## 2.1 Input methods

Students must be able to generate notes by selecting a class, board, subject, chapter, and topic from the library, or by typing a topic directly into a prompt box.

Support requests such as:

- Explain the complete chapter in detailed handwritten notes.
- Create revision notes for a particular topic.
- Explain every NCERT exercise question step by step.
- Make examination-focused notes for Class 10 Science.
- Generate detailed Class 11 Biology notes with labeled handwritten diagrams.
- Explain a difficult concept using simple language and real-life examples.
- Convert an uploaded PDF or supported document into organized study notes.
- Generate handwritten notes from a syllabus topic without inventing material outside the syllabus.

When a student chooses an entire chapter, generate the complete chapter according to the relevant syllabus rather than returning only a brief overview.

For large chapters, generate the notes in logically connected sections. Save each completed section and allow the student to continue generating the next section without losing previous work.

## 2.2 ChatGPT-quality explanations

The AI must write the notes as an excellent subject teacher would explain them to a student.

Begin with a simple explanation of the concept, establish the necessary background, and gradually introduce more advanced details. Explain why a concept works, not just what it means.

Use a natural teaching style that combines clear paragraphs, numbered steps, definitions, examples, comparisons, and relevant analogies. Explain technical terms when they first appear.

When appropriate, include:

- A simple definition followed by a detailed explanation.
- The underlying scientific, mathematical, or logical principle.
- Step-by-step derivations and calculations.
- Worked examples with explanations for every step.
- Relevant real-world applications.
- Important differences between similar concepts.
- Common misconceptions and examination mistakes.
- Important facts and formulas to memorize.
- NCERT-based questions with explained answers.
- Short-answer and long-answer examination questions.
- A concise revision summary at the end.

Avoid repetitive introductions, unnecessary filler, artificial motivational sentences, and unexplained textbook jargon.

Do not force every chapter into an identical template. Adapt the content structure to the topic and subject.

For numerical subjects, show the formula, known values, substitution, calculation, units, and final answer. For Biology, explain structures, functions, mechanisms, and relationships. For History, organize events chronologically and explain causes and consequences. For Computer Science, include algorithms, examples, pseudocode, and diagrams where useful.

## 2.3 Content completeness

The generator must determine the relevant chapter topics before creating the notes.

Create a chapter outline, map it to the official syllabus, and use that outline to generate the material section by section. Track which topics have been covered.

The system must not claim that a chapter is complete if required sections are missing.

Allow the student to choose between detailed learning notes, examination revision notes, and quick revision sheets. These must be genuinely different levels of detail rather than the same content with different headings.

## 2.4 AI provider integration

Implement a configurable AI service layer that can support the provider and model already used by the application, with the ability to change providers without rewriting the notes interface.

Keep provider API keys exclusively on the server. Validate model responses, handle rate limits, retry temporary failures, and display useful error messages.

Use structured content output for headings, paragraphs, equations, tables, diagram specifications, citations, and question sets. Validate the structure before saving it.

Do not assume that AI-generated content is automatically accurate. Add a verification process before presenting content as verified NCERT-aligned material.

# PART 3 — MAKE THE NOTES LOOK LIKE REAL HANDWRITING

This is the defining feature of the platform.

The system must create notes that look like carefully prepared handwritten notebook pages, not ordinary HTML paragraphs with a cursive font.

## 3.1 Notebook appearance

Render notes on realistic school notebook paper with subtle ruled lines, a natural left margin, handwritten headings, paragraphs, formulas, annotations, and page numbers.

Use blue or black pen for normal writing, red pen for selected corrections or warnings, and restrained highlighting for important information.

The handwriting should resemble human-written notes: natural character shapes, slightly varied letter spacing, small variations in baseline and stroke width, and realistic paragraph alignment.

Keep the handwriting readable and consistent. Avoid exaggerated distortion, artificial random rotation, overly decorative fonts, or messy text that makes studying difficult.

Provide a selection of handwriting styles, including neat student handwriting, clean teacher handwriting, and compact revision handwriting.

Use subtle paper texture and natural ink rendering. The paper must remain clean and readable rather than appearing artificially aged or excessively textured.

## 3.2 Genuine handwritten rendering

Build a handwriting renderer that converts the structured content generated by the AI into visual notebook pages.

The renderer must recognize the semantic role of each content element. A chapter heading, definition, paragraph, equation, table, diagram, and examination tip must not all be rendered in the same way.

Use suitable handwriting fonts as a foundation, with controlled variation in character spacing, line spacing, word placement, and ink appearance. Where supported, use genuine stroke-based handwriting rendering for selected elements.

Do not apply random transformations to entire paragraphs. Preserve readable text, mathematical alignment, and consistent page structure.

The output must be actual readable text rendered in a handwritten visual style, not a flattened decorative image with inaccessible or unsearchable content.

Maintain a structured content representation behind the rendered pages so that notes remain searchable, editable, accessible, and suitable for future regeneration.

## 3.3 Page layout

Build an intelligent notebook layout engine.

Calculate the height of paragraphs, headings, formulas, tables, and diagrams before placing them on the page. Respect page margins and available width.

When a section does not fit, move it to the next page naturally. Do not split a diagram, formula, or heading from its explanation in an awkward way.

Prevent text from overlapping ruled lines in ways that reduce legibility. Maintain adequate space around headings and diagrams.

Keep diagrams close to the explanations they support. Where a diagram needs more room, place it on a dedicated figure area or page.

Support continuous scrolling, page-by-page viewing, zoom, printing, and high-resolution PDF export.

The PDF output must preserve the handwriting appearance, diagram quality, page numbering, margins, and readable mathematical expressions.

## 3.4 Real notebook conventions

Use authentic educational note-taking conventions where appropriate:

- Handwritten chapter and topic headings.
- Underlined subheadings.
- Definitions emphasized with boxes or margins.
- Numbered steps for derivations.
- Formulas written on separate lines.
- Arrows connecting related ideas.
- Small explanatory annotations.
- Handwritten figure labels and captions.
- Important examination points marked with restrained emphasis.
- Worked examples separated from theoretical explanations.
- Revision summaries at the end of a topic.

These conventions must improve learning, not serve as random decoration.

# PART 4 — BUILD A REAL HAND-DRAWN DIAGRAM GENERATOR

Never substitute generic illustrations or stock artwork when a handwritten academic diagram is requested.

Create a dedicated diagram-generation system that translates structured academic diagram specifications into genuine hand-drawn-style strokes, shapes, arrows, and labels.

## 4.1 Diagram creation pipeline

For each topic requiring a diagram, the AI must first determine what the diagram should communicate.

It should generate a structured diagram specification containing the required components, their relationships, labels, connections, geometric constraints, and academic reference.

A rendering engine should then construct the diagram using SVG paths, canvas strokes, or an appropriate graphics library.

Apply controlled hand-drawn stroke variation to outlines and connectors while preserving the underlying scientific or mathematical geometry.

Render labels in a matching handwritten style, but keep them readable and correctly positioned.

The engine must not merely generate a text prompt and insert a random AI-generated image.

## 4.2 Subject-specific accuracy

For Biology, produce appropriate cell diagrams, organelles, tissue diagrams, anatomical structures, physiological systems, and other syllabus-relevant figures.

For Physics, produce ray diagrams, circuit diagrams, force diagrams, experimental setups, graphs, and field representations.

For Chemistry, produce chemical structures, electron-dot diagrams, reaction pathways, laboratory apparatus, and other suitable representations.

For Mathematics, produce geometric constructions, graphs, coordinate diagrams, and mathematical figures with correct geometric relationships.

For Computer Science, produce flowcharts, algorithm diagrams, database relationships, network topologies, and other technical figures.

Do not force a diagram into a chapter if it does not help explain the content.

## 4.3 Diagram positioning and labels

Create a reliable diagram layout engine with anchor points, bounding boxes, leader-line routing, and collision detection.

Labels must point to the correct components. Arrows must communicate the intended direction. Text must not overlap important structures, and the diagram must not extend beyond the page.

On smaller screens, adapt the layout without breaking the relationships between labels and structures.

Validate diagrams before publication. If automatic placement fails, retry layout or send the figure for editorial review.

## 4.4 Interactive diagrams

Where appropriate, allow students to zoom, pan, toggle labels, select structures, and reveal explanations.

Provide a practice mode that hides selected labels so students can test themselves.

When a student selects a label, highlight the corresponding structure and display its explanation.

Allow authorized editors to adjust labels and positions without rebuilding the entire diagram manually.

Maintain a structured, editable source for each diagram instead of saving only a flattened image.

# PART 5 — CREATE A COMPLETE CLASS-WISE NOTES LIBRARY

Build a persistent academic library that organizes content using this hierarchy:

**Class → Board or curriculum → Subject → Syllabus edition → Chapter → Topic → Notes → Diagrams → Practice material.**

Include support for Classes 6 through 12, with an architecture that can accommodate additional educational levels and entrance examinations.

Do not assume that every class has the same subjects, chapters, or syllabus. Populate the library according to the relevant curriculum and verified syllabus documents.

## 5.1 Library interface

Create a dedicated library page where students can browse classes, subjects, chapters, and topics.

Each class page must display available subjects. Each subject page must display the relevant chapters in syllabus order. Each chapter page must provide its notes, diagrams, explanations, and practice material.

Provide search by chapter name, topic, keyword, and formula where feasible.

Allow filtering by detailed notes, revision sheets, diagrams, solved examples, and practice questions.

Add recently viewed chapters, saved notes, bookmarks, reading progress, and chapter completion indicators.

Students should be able to return to the exact section or page they were studying.

## 5.2 Chapter content management

Every chapter must have a stable identifier and metadata containing its class, subject, curriculum, syllabus edition, source references, and verification status.

Authorized editors must be able to create, edit, review, approve, publish, archive, and revise notes.

Maintain version history. If a syllabus changes, identify affected chapters and flag them for review.

Do not display a chapter as verified unless it has passed the appropriate checks.

If content has not yet been prepared, display an honest preparation status instead of fabricated notes.

# PART 6 — NCERT AND RELIABLE BOOK-BASED CONTENT

Accuracy is mandatory. The platform must distinguish verified source material from AI-generated explanations that have not yet been reviewed.

Use official NCERT textbooks, official CBSE syllabus documents, examination sample papers, and official examination notices as primary references when applicable.

Supplement them with established subject references such as H. C. Verma for relevant Physics topics, Campbell Biology for supplementary Biology explanations, and appropriate standard Chemistry and Mathematics textbooks.

Use official examination authority websites for dates and rules.

The selected reference must match the student's class, subject, syllabus, and examination requirements.

For every chapter, map topics to the relevant syllabus sections. Maintain references to the sources used, including edition or publication information where available.

When the system has access to legitimate source documents, retrieve the relevant material and use it to ground the explanations. When it does not, clearly identify the limitation instead of pretending that the content was verified against a textbook.

Do not fabricate quotations, textbook page numbers, official announcements, citations, or examination dates.

Paraphrase educational content in original language. Respect copyright restrictions and do not reproduce entire textbooks or other protected works without authorization.

Include a clear distinction between official syllabus coverage, supplementary explanations, and AI-generated examples.

